  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`, which parses `key=value` into a `(key, value)` tuple. Input with no `=` or an empty key fails with `'<value>' is not a valid key=value pair.` The full test suite passes (1916 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed).\n\nChanges:\n- **`src/click/types.py`**: new `KeyValue(ParamType)` class after `Tuple`. It splits on the first `=` only, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`, and `a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`. It also passes through input that is already a tuple.\n- **`src/click/__init__.py`**: exports `KeyValue`.\n- **`tests/test_types.py`**: tests for valid parsing, invalid input, tuple pass-through, and an end-to-end `@click.option(\"--set\", type=click.KeyValue(), multiple=True)` run.\n- **`docs/api.md`**: added an `autoclass` entry next to `Tuple`.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- Whitespace is not stripped, so `\" a = b\"` gives `(\" a \", \" b\")`.\n- The class is documented as `versionadded:: 8.6`, since 8.5.0 is the latest released version in `CHANGES.md`. I did not add a changelog entry.",
